WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.520
RAG,
or Retrieval Augmented Generation,

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:06.620
retrieves relevant documents to
give context to an LLM,

3
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.840
and this makes it
much better at answering

4
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.620
queries and performing tasks.

5
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.460
Many teams are
using simple retrieval

6
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.740
techniques based on
semantic similarity or embeddings,

7
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.420
but you learn more
sophisticated techniques in this

8
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.860
course, which will let you
do much better than that.

9
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.140
A common workflow in RAG is to take
your query and embed that,

10
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.240
then find the most similar documents,
meaning ones with similar embeddings,

11
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.540
and that's the context. But
the problem with that

12
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:34.960
is that it can tend to find documents
that talk about similar

13
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.000
topics as a query, but
not actually contain the answer. But

14
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.680
you can take the initial
user query and rewrite.

15
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.520
This is called query expansion.

16
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.700
Rewrite it to pull
in more directly related documents.

17
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.740
Two key related techniques. One,
to expand the optional

18
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.160
query into multiple queries
by rewording or rewriting

19
00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.660
it in different ways. And second,
to even guess or hypothesize what

20
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.880
the answer might look like to
see if we can find anything

21
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.380
in our document
collection that looks more like an

22
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.620
answer rather than
only generally talking

23
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.800
about the topics of the query.

24
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.920
I'm delighted that your
instructor for this course is Anton

25
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:14.888
Troynikov.

26
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.440
Anton has been one of the
innovators driving forward

27
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.080
the state-of-the-art and retrieval
for AI applications.

28
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.100
He is co-founder of Chroma, which
provides one of the

29
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.240
most popular open-source vector
databases.

30
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.080
If you've taken one of our
LangChain short courses taught

31
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.180
by Harrison Chase, you
have very likely used Chroma.

32
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:35.620
Thank you, Andrew.

33
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.100
I'm really excited to be working
with you on this course

34
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.860
and share what I'm seeing
out in the field in terms

35
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.460
of what does and doesn't work in
RAG deployments.

36
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.500
We'll start off the course
by doing a quick

37
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.960
review of RAG applications.

38
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.600
You will then learn
about some of the

39
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.940
pitfalls of retrieval where
simple vector search doesn't

40
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:51.460
do well.

41
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.920
Then you'll learn several methods to
improve the results.

42
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.280
As Andrew mentioned,
the first methods use an

43
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.440
LLM to improve the query itself.

44
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.680
Another method re-ranks
query results with help from something

45
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.660
called a cross encoder, which
takes in a pair

46
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.940
of sentences and produces
a relevancy score.

47
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.640
You'll also learn how to adapt the
query embeddings based on user feedback

48
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.300
to produce more
relevant results.

49
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.980
There's a lot of innovation going
on in RAG right now. So

50
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.820
in the final lesson, we'll also
go over some of the cutting edge techniques

51
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.340
that aren't mainstream yet
and are only just now appearing

52
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.020
in research.

53
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.780
And I think they'll
become much more mainstream soon.

54
